<BaseLayout title="Face Acupressure Points" description="Discover facial acupressure points including Yintang, Taiyang, and LI20. Learn how to relieve sinus pressure, headaches, and eye strain with targeted point stimulation." breadcrumbs={[{"label":"Home","href":"/"},{"label":"Body Atlas","href":"/atlas/"},{"label":"Face","href":"/body/face-acupressure-points/"}]}>
<article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
  <div class="text-center mb-10">
    <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">Body Region</span>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mt-2">Face</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">Face acupressure works with points you already know by shape — Yintang between the brows, Taiyang at the temples, LI20 beside the nostrils. Gentle fingertip work on these and other facial points features in guides for headache, sinus congestion, eye strain, jaw tension, and anxiety.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-32 h-36"><ellipse cx="50" cy="50" rx="32" ry="40" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></ellipse><circle cx="36" cy="40" r="3" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><circle cx="64" cy="40" r="3" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><path d="M40 55 Q50 62 60 55" fill="none" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4"></path><circle cx="50" cy="55" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="50" cy="35" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="42" cy="50" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="58" cy="50" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><text x="50" y="105" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Face</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Face Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The face is the most visible surface on the body's point map, and it is also where several common complaints live. Yintang, the spot between the eyebrows, is the classic choice for anxiety, stress, insomnia, and headache in TCM practice, and a natural place to start for people new to the whole system. Taiyang, at the temples, and GB14 (Yangbai), above the brows, are traditional responses to headache, migraine-type pressure, and eye strain. LI20 (Yingxiang) beside the nostrils and ST2 (Sibai) below the eye appear routinely in routines for sinus congestion and facial pain, while ST6 (Jiache) at the jaw addresses clenching and TMJ-type tension. These same points recur in our headache, sleep, anxiety, and sinus guides.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Facial points sit on the same meridian channels that run through the rest of the body, so classical practice treats them with the same logic as points elsewhere. But the face's nerves and muscles sit close to the surface, and they hold a great deal of everyday tension from screens, stress, and clenching. That is why facial work favors a light, precise touch: you still aim for the deep, dull deqi sensation, just reached with far less pressure than the legs or feet demand.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Key Points in This Region</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-5">Six points across the brow, temples, nose, cheeks, and jaw. All are pressed with clean, short nails and a gentler touch than in any other body region.</p>
