  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Face Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Use the pad of the index or middle finger at ninety degrees to the skin, and keep your nails short. Increase pressure very slowly over ten to fifteen seconds until you feel a soft, deep ache, then hold for thirty to sixty seconds with light, even breathing — facial points take a shorter hold than the one-to-two-minute standard used elsewhere. Never press the eyeballs, inside the nostrils, or onto raw skin. Work both sides evenly and release slowly.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is Yintang safe to press?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yintang, the point between the eyebrows just above the bridge of the nose, is widely regarded as one of the safest and most beginner-friendly points in acupressure practice. Light fingertip pressure for thirty to sixty seconds with even breathing is the standard approach. Skip it if the area is irritated or if you have had recent facial surgery or injury. As with all facial work, the eyes and nostrils stay untouched, and clinical anxiety or insomnia still deserves professional guidance.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can face acupressure help sinus congestion?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI20 (Yingxiang), at the side of each nostril, and ST2 (Sibai), just below the eye, are the points most often suggested in TCM practice when congestion is the complaint. Two fingertips pressing upward and outward for thirty to sixty seconds is a common, gentle technique. It is a comfort practice rather than a decongestant, and it cannot clear an infection or allergy by itself. Persistent sinus pain or fever should be reviewed by a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold facial points for thirty to sixty seconds — notably shorter than the one-to-two-minute standard for the rest of the body — because the skin and muscles of the face are delicate. Use light pressure and short nails, breathe evenly, and repeat softly a few times a day if you like. Release slowly, and never press on the eyes or inside the nostrils.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
